# Repository Governance

## Adaptive Vehicle Intelligence Architecture Engineering Repository – Public (AER-PU)

---

## Purpose

The purpose of this governance document is to establish the policies, principles, responsibilities, and processes that govern the creation, review, approval, publication, maintenance, and evolution of engineering knowledge within the Adaptive Vehicle Intelligence Architecture Engineering Repository – Public (AER-PU).

This document ensures that the repository remains a trusted, consistent, traceable, and authoritative source of approved engineering knowledge throughout the lifecycle of the AVIA project.

---

# Governance Objectives

This governance framework exists to:

- Maintain the integrity of the repository.
- Ensure engineering documentation remains accurate and traceable.
- Establish consistent engineering documentation practices.
- Define the lifecycle of repository documents.
- Support disciplined systems engineering throughout the AVIA project.
- Preserve engineering knowledge for future development.

---

# Governance Principles

The repository shall operate according to the following principles:

### GP-001
Repository governance takes precedence over individual repository documents.

### GP-002
Only reviewed and approved engineering knowledge shall be published.

### GP-003
Every published document shall have a defined purpose.

### GP-004
Every engineering document shall be uniquely identifiable.

### GP-005
Every engineering decision shall be traceable.

### GP-006
Repository content shall evolve through controlled reviews and approved baselines.

### GP-007
Historical engineering knowledge shall be preserved through version control rather than replacement.

### GP-008
Repository governance shall remain technology-independent whenever possible.

---

# Governance Scope

This governance applies to:

- Repository documentation
- Engineering specifications
- Architectural documents
- Engineering principles
- Engineering laws
- Validation reports
- Technical diagrams
- Published engineering knowledge

This governance does not apply to:

- Private engineering work
- Draft concepts
- Brainstorming material
- Internal discussions
- Experimental documentation

These belong within the private engineering repository.

---

# Repository Authority

Within the Public Repository, the following order of authority shall apply:

1. Repository Governance
2. Repository Policies
3. Approved Engineering Baselines
4. Published Engineering Documents
5. Supporting Documentation

In the event of conflict, higher authority documents take precedence.

---

# Governance Responsibilities

Repository governance is responsible for ensuring:

- Documentation quality
- Engineering consistency
- Traceability
- Controlled publication
- Baseline management
- Version management
- Repository organization

---

# Governance Philosophy

The repository is not merely a storage location for engineering documents.

It is an engineering knowledge system whose purpose is to preserve, organize, and communicate approved engineering knowledge in a structured and traceable manner.

---

# Governance Lifecycle

Every engineering document shall progress through the following lifecycle:

Draft

↓

Engineering Review

↓

Revision

↓

Approval

↓

Baseline

↓

Publication

↓

Maintenance

↓

Archive (if superseded)

---

# Compliance

Every document published within the Public Repository shall comply with this governance framework.

Documents that do not comply shall not be considered official repository publications.

---

# Continuous Improvement

This governance document may evolve through controlled engineering review.

Every approved revision shall create a new governance baseline while preserving previous versions for historical traceability.

